Date: Sat, 20 Nov 93 10:04:52 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Freezing Gametes

a sperm bank recently advertised in the MIT newspaper, and a phone call 
left me quite intrigued. they have a minimum height requirement, health 
exam, and since they are scanning the mit population, they are presuming 
to catch high SAT-types.

only 7-10% of the male population have semen that "freeze well." in 
order to determine this characteristic, which is apparently independent 
of other physical traits, they need to take a sample, and freeze it for 
at least a month.

the implications are: 1) if sperm donors end up being overrepresented in 
future generations, then this strange quality of "freezing well" cd turn 
out to be the single most adaptive feature of humans. 

2) freezing well is likely to be a pleitropic feature, which might be 
perversely correlated with other characteristics which would then 
dominate the population.

a general question: although becoming a donor is clearly the single 
smartest thing you could do as a biological organism, does maximizing 
inclusive fitness align with how others view the extropian code?

i am aware that a facile yes conflates biological optima with other 
planes of evaluation. even if one is motivated to act selfishly, the 
question remains open whether this implies that one would be furthering 
selfishness by trying to reproduce as much as technologically possible.  

