From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: ANARCHY

I was active in LP politics for a while, and we managed to get 20%
of the vote for our state representative candidate.  This was the
highest vote% for a Libertarian in the Pacfic Northwest.  The vast
majority of Libertarian candidates get 0-5%.  But while relatively
spectacular vs. other LP candidates, 20% is still nowhere close to winning.

The California school choice initiative, similar to the one passed in
Wisconsin, upon being vilified as a "libertarian" initiative by its 
opponents, had its campaign funding dry up, and lost by a landslide, c. 
70%-30%.  Such a stunning rout is rare in the history of public 
initiatives.  Even the much hyped "Libertarian" state legislators in New 
Hampshire actually got elected as Democrats and Republicans, while also 
carrying the LP label.  There are a handful of Libertarian liquor 
commissioners, constables, dog-catchers, and the like.  In the LP U.S. 
Presidential ticket vote share has gone way downhill since 1980's feeble 
1%. The LP has a long-standing reputation as a fringe whacko party, 
running Hustler Busty Beauty centerfolds, punks, hippies, etc. who get 
get the 1% fringe whacko vote, and nothing any idealists 
have done for decades, and little we might do now, can change that.  
Furthermore, even if a miracle happened and we won important 
elections, they might be hollow victories, for most important politics 
takes place outside the Hallowed Halls of Congress: business protocols, 
technology standards, personal technological capabilities, etc.

I don't dismiss electoral politics as an important adjunct for
our political efforts, but it is silly to make a process that
has such little effect the main focal point of the efforts of
so many well-meaning political idealists.  In answer to
the original question, some of us have found much more 
effective ways to bring our own world closer to anarchy, as I 
described in my Libertarian Realpolitik post of a few months ago 
(enclosed, rerun warning!)

Newsgroups: alt.politics.libertarian
Subject: Libertarian Realpolitik

I appreciate L. Neil Smith's article on libertarian and LP
direction.  Alas, like many others trying to break
into contemporary U.S. politics, he emphasizes the
politically weak but expensive efforts to win at ritual
elections.  Those efforts are still important, especially
at the local levels where we stand a chance of winning
and making a difference.  But elections play a role 
more ritual than critical in modern politics.  Here's
a different perspective, some visions from the growing
Libertarian Realpolitik movement, which focuses on making
freedom for ourselves in today's world instead of merely
dreaming of the day we can get a majority, or build ourselves
space colonies to get away from our Earthside oppressors,
or other common futile libertarian science fictioneering.

Libertarian Realpolitik is like the LP is inspired by von Mises, 
Hayek, Rand, Heinlein, and Rothbard but based in the real
world and practical applications as found in Harry Browne,
_How I Found Freedom in an Unfree World_, Mark Skousen, etc.
Realpolitik finds a wide variety of tools more potent than
the lever in the ballot box: the phone, the fax, the PC with PGP, 
the ISIL booths on college campuses, and the _practice_ of keeping 
and bearing arms, whether our nosy, greedy, self-righteous
neighbors, who have spent far more time listening to TV
stars praise the Brady Bill than listening to us, think we have 
the right or not.

"Crypto-anarchy", a term coined by cypherpunk co-founder and 
libertarian Tim May, is a Galt's Gulch scenario where many
libertarians take economic privacy in their own hands and "blip
off the radar screen" by moving into the electronic information economy,
implementing a securely private economic information and monetary
system hidden by viels of cryptography from the modern robbers
of the information highway.

It's a powerful concept, far more powerful (but complementary
too) the traditional physical Galt's Gulch concept, currently
embodied in Eric Klein's "Oceania", an attempt to form a new
country consisting of free banks, medical clinics, etc. on offshore 
platforms.  At first glance crypto-anarchy looks just as much like 
science fiction as the other dreams, a mad combination of
William Gibson and Ayn Rand, except for one big 
difference -- it's being built today.  Check out the Wired
and Village Voice articles on "cypherpunks".  Check out the
cypherpunks mailing list (use cypherpunks-request@toad.com).
Today hackers, bankers, lawyers, and on-line service vendors
of a libertarian or anarchic bent are banding together to build
an on-line Galt's Gulch.  No reason to wait, no reason to
daydream, no need to make speeches -- just start hacking.  
PGP is all over the globe despite U.S. government's efforts to 
shut it down; anonymous remailers are being deployed.  "Cypherpunks 
write code."

This is no "competent people take control" Galt Strike fantasy.
The people competent at taking control are already in control,
but that doesn't mean they're competent at other things, and
it certainly doesn't mean they're libertarian.  A more likely 
scenario is an _emergent_ Galt strike: tax avoidance on a massive 
scale motivated not by political ideals but by personal self-interest 
and change of habit as dodging the Information Highway 
Robbers becomes easy.  Crypto-anarchy in the information
economy complements a decentralized Oceania in the physical
economy, a world of "perpetual tourists" who
need not tie themselves to the increasingly avoidable and
insane territorial states.

Crypto-anarchy is only one of the flashier aspects of Libertarian
Realpolitik.  More mundane but important aspects include
protecting your privacy by using cash where possible, and
bearer ATM or credit cards where electronic transfer is needed,
making oneself "judgement proof" from our runaway system
of confiscatory taxation, civil forfeiture, and runaway
tort judgements, learning to use the tools of the information
age to coordinate political action, and learning how
to keep and bear modern arms with which to defend one's
physical property.

Nor is Electioneering vs. Realpolitik an either/or issue.
Realpolitik does not reject electioneering; it merely puts
it in its proper role of one political tool among many.
For crypto-anarchy we need talented hackers working on PGP interfaces, 
remailers, digicash, on-line markets, etc.  We also need many bankers
and commercial lawyers setting up privacy-enhanced businesses.  
We also need a Libertarian Party and other efforts at education 
and political activism.  But I suggest these should be coordinated: 
the business practices can be done with libertarian politics in mind,
and the LP should put more emphasis on business practice
and technology as a potent sphere of political activism,
and less emphasis on electoral politics.  Furthermore
LP's lobbying can be more focused on those areas
that enable politically independent business: eliminate
crypto export restrictions and governmental key escrow schemes,
RKBA, school vouchers to allow independent education, etc.

Instead of an Oceania which can be taken out by a single 
Exocet or Tomahawk, let's put first things first and develop a 
voluntary libertarian militia and legal defense fund to defend our 
political supporters' property rights across jurisdictions.

What I'm suggesting here is a Libertarian RealPolitik
that combines the traditional LP with business interests and
economic privacy, led by a Libertarian Party that gets things done for 
its supporters in the real world instead of wasting so many 
contributions on futile attempts to win in the religious rituals of 
the ballot box.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

