Date: Sat, 20 Nov 93 11:27:40 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Investment, Philanthropy, and Oceania

Several folks are interested in start-ups and how to get them going.
Derek Zahn has proposed a VirtSem (virtual seminar, in the parlance we
used for the successful AIT VirtSem last spring). R. David Murray is
also apparently interested, as is Nick Szabo and probably others.


Hence the title of this thread.

In this post I'll describe an investment I recently made in a
start-up company. I rarely discuss my finances in public, but Mark
Venture's posting about funding Oceania and the LP in general causes me
to say more.

I want to show in what ways I put my money where my mouth is.

Mark Venture writes:

> 	I find it VERY curious that Extropians should be so skeptical
> of Eric Klien's Oceania project.  I'm not sure if it is resentment that
> someone ELSE could beat Extropians to creating a libertarian community,
> or that they don't approve of his methods.  I, for one, am cautiously
> optimistic, though I won't hold my breath (or donate money, though I did
> help a tiny bit in correcting errors of its Constitution).

As one of the most vocal (keyboardal?) skeptics, let me reiterate that
it is _not_ resentment. If Oceania succeeds, we'll all be better off.

I just consider it a pipe dream, like teenagers drawing pictures of
space ships and organizing "companies" to colonize space. As Perry and
others noted, a multibillion dollar project demands certain
precursors: significant commitment by investors, involvement of large
engineering firms (shipbuilders, docks, etc.), and a comprehensive
financial plan. These seem to be absent here, based on what I have
seen and heard discussed.

That Mark will not even invest, let alone "donate" (ugh!) money is
telling. A project like Oceania will sink if built as "charityware."

Me, I _do_ in fact put money into some ventures I think have some
reasonable chance of succeeding. Several weeks I ago I wrote out a
check for $62,500 to buy founder's stock (Class A Preferred) in a
company doing wireless localization and rangefinding, a company that
has developed a novel use of nonsinusoidal, ultrawideband
electromagnetic radiation.

(This is a relatively small investment, but, considering the
risk-reward figures, is all I was willing to make.)

There's probably something like a 70% chance my $62K will evaporate
almost completely, a 20% chance I'll get back more or less what I put
in, and perhaps a 10% chance my $62K iunvestment will turn into a
million bucks or more. The risk-return graph again.

And I did not simply invest because the product sounded neat. Rather,
I've known the team for years, I reviewed their plan, visited their
lab a number of times, studied spread-spectrum textbooks, asked
vicious and penetrating questions (so they told me, and thanked me
for), talked to other investors, and generally assured myself that my
money would be spent to actually produce a product that would begin to
produce profits directly or would at least make the company a
tantalizing enough enterprise that the company would be valued highly
enough for me to make a tidy profit.

This is real world of writing out checks for $62,500 in real money and
perhaps never seeing the money again.

I won't invest money in Oceania, or space companies, or even in
nanotech because I see no "closure" between investing and obtaining a
reasonable chance for a return on my investment. Giving money to
Drexler or to Foresight is _not_ an investment. Ditto for Oceania.

If Oceania were to be a floating casino, say, with funding by Trump,
Murdoch, and that sort, and engineering plans were really proceeding
apace, then it might conceivably be worth investing in--for me. But
since I know nothing about gambling laws, about the laws governing
offshore "pirate" facilities (such as: government seizure of vessels
in international waters, which happens routinely), about ocean
engineering, and about many other related items, I _still_ wouldn't
get near this.

That they have a nice constitution (some say, others disagree) is,
well, _nice_. But that's not saying much.

I am still looking for interesting opportunities in crypto and
cyberspace, but nothing tangible has appeared. Sure, a lot of folks
would welcome financing, but I'm not looking to be a philanthropist.

(I have major problems with the _philanthropic_ approach so many free
market libertarians seem to relish. In the space business, they're
always on the lookout for "angels," the term for rich folks who will
make ideologically-driven donations and investments. In my opinion, a
bad idea. Philanthropically driven ventures lack the market incentives
needed to really bring a product to market quickly. Instead, the
founders get to dilly-dally for years, while losing their edge. This
is partly what sunk both AMIX and Xanadu--in their case, the "angel"
was Autodesk, which let them plan and strategize and whatnot for far
too long. Ironically, quick-start, evolution-driven alternatives like
WWW and ClariNet got started long after them and yet are now
thriving.)

Back to Mark's comments:

> 	Anyway...if you would like to know what *I* am doing about the
> problem of government...I am a fairly active member of the Libertarian
> Party.  (Down Tim! Down! :-)  I'm Secretary/Treasurer of the Niagara

Why would I object to Mark spending his time on something he likes? 

> Frontier Libertarian Party, a newly organized local group centered
> around Buffalo, NY.  I see promise in the progress that the New Hampshire
> LP has had (four Libs elected to the State Legislature, one appointed
> to the state liquor commission).  However, I should point out that I see
> the LP as a *long-term* aid to combating government.  As Tim May has
> correctly pointed out, it took 22 years for the LP to get as far as it has,
> which is not staggering.  The strategy for liberty needs much more help
> to be sure.

The changes that are already happening are primarily _technological_:
the growing irrelevance of national borders and of local politics, the
cyberspace infrastructure, strong cryptography, and so on.

> 	If libertarianism is in the future of Earth politics, I believe
> this will come about through a *variety* of sources and influences.
> I don't think that we can predict with confidence how it will happen.
> Tim seems to think that cryptography is the answer [though cryptography
> was first invented more than 2000 years ago (the Caesar cypher) and not
> one libertarian country has resulted since then as a result, the LP looks

Touche, but modern cryptography, implemented directly on computers, is
of course dramatically different from Caeser and Vignere ciphers.

I have shared some of what I am doing, as Mark suggests we do.

A final comment:

Please don't write to me asking for funding. I worked hard for my
money and don't relish the thought of seeing it vanish, as most
startup investments do. In the case of my investment cited above, I
watched my friends invest nearly 4 years of their life--8
person-years--in getting the basics completed first. I saw them
negotiate with patent attorneys (several patents are in the works
already), with law firms (official papers), and with other venture
capitalists. They also recently got an ARPA contract, about which I
should not say more. All of these things convinced me they had done
the "due diligence" that is needed.

(They financed the 3-4 years they put into this from their savings.
They had designed accelerator chips for PC companies, switching
fabrics and DSPs for AT&T, and were of course very well-paid. This
experience also gave them the track record, the ability to design
their own VLSI chips, and contacts throughout the industry.)

Many startups can get going with far less effort and money, if the
idea is right. The latest "MacWorld" issue (December) has a nice piece
by Guy Kawasaki on how two guys developed the "DiskDoubler" product in
two months, showed it at "ComputerWare," the famous Mac-only store in
Palo Alto (and where I've been shopping since 1986), and were off and
running. They sold out to Fifth Generation a while back and are now
multimillionaires. It can happen. Just not very often.

(The "Practical Realism" hand beats the "Boundless Optimism" hand
nearly every time.)

If you need $10,000 to fund your startup, and you're convinced your
idea is sound, then such a small sum should be easy to raise. Parents,
mortgages on houses, etc.

(My guess is that most people who think $10K or $30K is enough to
start their company haven't really planned things out. What most
people I talk to who want this kind of funding have in mind is getting
enough money to allow them not to work for a year or so while they
_think_ of something to sell! Bad idea! Very bad!)

Well, I hope this has given you a slightly different insight into how
I do in my own way put my money where my mouth is. (Though I keep most
of my money in presumably safer investments....I don't want to ever
have to go back to a normal job.)

I hope this ties in with the thread on start-ups that Derek Zahn, R.
David Murray, Nick Szabo, and others have expressed interest in.

Cheers, 


--Tim May


