There's little I can add to Tim's good post about Oceania and his
recent investment (on which I wish him luck).  I agree with his
assessment of Oceania.

I would like to focus on a slightly different aspect of "Start-Ups"
than Tim did.  Having little money myself, I am more interested
in the very-small-startup concept, as of course not all business
ventures need years of work by a dedicated team or hundreds of
thousands of dollars (not to mention billions) to get going.  Free-
marketeers often talk about the virtues of such small efforts --
the archetypical entrepreneurial peasant carting potatoes to the
city to sell in the open-air market.

I believe that the continuing expansion of computing, networking,
and the information economy creates many such opportunities for
individuals and small groups looking for entrepreneurial niches.
Many of us will participate in more than one such venture, creating
multiple income sources for ourselves.  Personally, I am spending all
of my time attempting to make this exact concept work for me (my only
advice so far is that if the implementation of an idea seems to be
at about the right level of difficulty for the resources you have
available, it's probably actually too difficult!  I am beginning
to wonder whether my major effort at present isn't biting off more
than I can chew).

This isn't anything new, really.  For example, a freelance writer
fits the model of the tiny start-up quite well:  buy a typewriter
and some paper, and get to work.  1993 version:  buy a computer
and a modem, and get to work.  Of course, just as a freelance
writer had better have some skills putting ideas on paper, so
the freelance computerist had better have comparable skills.

I am most interested, when considering this concept theoretically,
in the prospects for driving the time between modest idea and
functioning computer application (software development being my
personal marketable specialty) downward as much as possible,
making "lightning software development" a reality.  This isn't
too uncommon right now on a "custom" basis, but I wonder how
possible it would be in a context more like a painter who
paints paintings and then tries to sell them in the marketplace.
That is, if Romana Machado decided to sell her new program "Stego",
would there be any buyers?  Does anybody know if there is a functioning
infrastructure for matching up people with needs to the "mini-apps"
that are becoming more and more common?  I know that "shareware"
exists, and contrary to most people I think it works quite well
for all concerned, but it's still too hard for people to find the
programs they want.

I like this model for a freelance computerist rather better than
the "contractor" model because it leaves room for the tiny
entrepreneur to implement his or her own vision and sell it
rather than implementing the vision of a customer with a
specific need (and often having only one or two people ever
even use the resulting program).  Does it sound possible?

derek


