Date: Sat, 20 Nov 1993 15:03:05 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Investment, Philanthropy, and Oceania

This is a delightful thread. Here's some of one man's opinions on startups
(somewhat disjointed due to the flu).

Let's start with the flamebait (in no particular order). 

   (1)  I'm flatly against philanthropy for developing a business. 
   (2)  I believe that "arrogant ignorance" kills many startups. 
   (3)  Business success typically depends on an "execution" play, 
        not brilliant ideas. 
   (4)  Managing people well is a skill-set many engineers do not posses,
        and in fact scorn (this costs money).
   (5)  Starting a business is a no-brainer, becoming profitable can be
        a brain-drainer.
   (6)  Building a sustaining organization is often team effort, and more 
        often than not, good executives have a history of being team 
        players -- have played organized sports or played in an 
        orchestra/band where group success depended on individual
        contribution and cooperation in real time.
   (7)  There's more opportunity than time, and distraction is a killer.
   (8)  Ventures that are hard to fund, shouldn't be funded -- the mix
        of vision/mission, resources, and management is probably bad.
   (9)  Smart money typically is.
   (10) Manufacturing - Marketing = Scrap.
   (11) By entering the arena, you immediately become someone else's
        opponent and they may well take it as their mission to put you
        out of their misery...
   (12) The object of business is profit.
   (11) [That's enough to begin with.]

I've been working in and around start-up businesses for over 20 years. My
first one was a pancake and was tossed away in three months. Two others
I've started have done very, very well (each sold for a comfortable purse
after being a cash-cow for years). Some I've consulted with or work with
haven't done so well. Other clients have done very well. Experiences with
these later two groups lead to the flamebait abstracted above.

Side note -- recommended reading.
  (1)  Anything by Michael Porter, esp. _Competitive Advantage_.
  (2)  _Selling the Dream_ by Guy Kawasaki
  (3)  _Crossing the Chasm_ by (I think) Geoffry Moore
  (4)  _Guerilla xxx [marketing, PR..]_ can't remember authors
  (5)  _One Minute Manager_ by Blanchard
  (6)  _The Autodesk File_ by John Walker
  (7)  _Business Week_
  (8)  _Before the Battle_ by Edward Flanagan
  (9)  _Seven Habits of Highly Effective People_ by Steven Covey
  (10) _Getting it Right the Second Time_ by Michael Gershman

I heard Paul Saffo of the Institute of the Future make a pair of great remarks:
  (a)  Never mistake a clear vision for a short distance.
  (b)  Venture Capitalists rest like babies -- they sleep for two hours
       and wake up crying.

Tim May posted a great insight in his "Rapture of the Future can be Cured."
Roughly paraphrased, he pointed out that many "free market types" are
unemployed, work in minimum wage jobs, in non-profits, or are waiting for
the government to fund their latest idea. 

Many of the "free-marketers" I personally know are completely inexperienced
regarding making money and building a profitable enterprise. Their theories
are in the ozone. Often, these people express their idea for "business" as
being simple and express anger and frustration at potential investors for
not seeing the obvious, but draw a blank when asked "who will buy," "what
will they pay," "what are the barriers to competitive market entry," or
"what's the burdened cost of a sale." Then they get upset when investors
look away.

In my experience, startup business is VERY hard work, and often takes a
certain personality type to succeed. Once the burn rate starts (i.e., you
have expenses), the time for debate and lingering discussion over. Business
success requires action, not reflection. An 80% solution that gets to
market is always better (in business terms) than the 90% solution that's a
year late or the 100% solution that completely misses the window of
opportunity. It's a race, the it goes to the swift.

I believe the primary reason for success or failure of startup companies is
the founding management. If they're committed to a vision, instead of the
business, they're probably in the wrong place and academia or a non-profit
would be better. I've come to learn that one the key success
characteristics of people in risky enterprises is knowing when to cut
losses, and doing it quickly; have a clear focus and not getting
distracted; and having a clear picture of where the market is today, not in
the great by-and-by.

Some entrepreneur wannabes are ignorant of market reality, and sufficiently
arrogant that potential alliance partners, investors and customers aren't
encouraged to take the risk of possibly dumping their cash down the tube. 

The public doesn't take huge steps of behavior change too often, especially
with technology solutions. (Yes, oral contraceptives, BIC pens, electronic
calculators are notable exceptions). It's not that investors don't want to
bet on completely new ideas and much as investors rarely want to bet on
untried and inexperienced management. People without money rarely know how
to manage it.

And make no mistake, in this day of niche marketing, the world ain't gonna
beat a path to your door. Effective (and costly) marketing, advertising,
and PR are going to be necessary. Get qualified people to do these things.

Both the size of the potential market and the relative market share that
can be attained are boundary values on success. It's easier to penetrate a
growing market than one that's mature, but there's a greater number of
competitors and its a grueling race to get established. So answer these
questions: what are the total projected sales of X over the next 3-5 years?
What share can be grabbed and maintained? At what cost? Be explicit about
the assumptions.

Keep in mind that R&D costs money, lotsa money, and administration,
clerical and support are all very costly expenses. All those who see
themselves at the top of the food chain are burdensome overhead. The sales
people pay everybody's salaries. Be nice to them; pay them well.

Remember that benefits sell and features don't.

Packaging and positioning are crucial. Identify a niche, do enough market
research to have a clear picture of benefits to the target market, deliver
and relentlessly pursue it.

Streamline internal operations as much as possible. Many startups are
inefficient when it comes to order processing and making sales. Simplify
every step from getting a lead to closing the sale: have a standardized
system for lead tracking, qualify/triage leads, have snappy fulfillment,
pursue with followup, and keep meticulous track of all this stuff. Be
relentless.

Be good to workers. Make sure they have comfortable and healthy conditions:
good lighting, ergonomic furniture, the best tools. 

As the entrepreneur, do not tolerate anybody who isn't a team player. Flush
them immediately; same goes for anyone who harasses fellow workers, is
surly, unreliable or tardy. Morale counts much more in a startup than in
larger firms. Do everything possible to keep it at a peak. Get rid of the
bad seeds immediately. (Better yet, learn how to hire good ones.)

Don't wait too long to hire a cracker-jack secretary or administrative
assistant. Have him/her answer the phones, handle correspondence, run
errands, make copies, organize the filing system, keep the office running.
Business means an office system; the entrepreneurs time shouldn't be wasted
on this stuff. Profitable business is a two fold execution play: inside and
out. Make the office run as a model of efficiency; make the marketing a
sales do the same.

Practice closing sales, then practice some more, then do it more. This is
where the cash comes from.

Look for alliances and "big wins" that are mutually beneficial. Pursue
them. Build large networks, continually look for linkages where results are
multiplied many times over relative to the effort input.

Keep in mind that there's always more opportunity than time, more work than
can get done, that perfectionists starve, and speed is life (to quote Tom
Peters). Set priorties, stick to them.

Have an exit strategy.

....

These are some opinions I've developed working around startups. I've got
loads more, but it's now time to head to Berkeley for some social events
and the Firesign Theater.

