Subject: LANG: General Semantics et al

Max Black brilliantly characterized one of the main points in general
semantics:  

"We petrify linguistic analogy into metaphysical dogma."

This captures even better than "the map is not the territory" the bug
people engage in, and it captures a sense of the negative
consequences.  

Linguistic analogies include many uses of the dreaded "be" verb, but
they also include the active, flowery passages that build an image in
the listeners mind, incidental adjectives that relegate their noun to
some general category that can then be dismissed, etc. (One can also
generalize "linguistic analogies" to include other forms of
communication, of course).

Petrification starts when people use these analogies too freely, and
start assuming properties of the analogies (the map) apply to the
target of the analogy (the territory).  If "politicians are stupid",
then we might ignore their low cunning at manipulating mobs :-).  The
connotations of petrificataion suggests the ability of humans to get
entrenched in their analogies, seeing the world through filters that
color everything in a non-realistic world-view.

The "metaphysical dogma" suggests the religious wars that have been
fought over different imagined models of the world, but it also
applies to the simple impositions that people make in everyday life;
if they hear that "I am a programmer" might assume that I wear
glasses, drink coke, am under-socialized, might not have showered, am
over-weight, etc.  Few people make these assumptions explicitly; they
just have a model in their head, and feel a twinge of surprise when
they meet me in person (only one or two of the above assumed
properties apply to me).  Note that saying "I program computers" in
E-prime style helps only a little.

General semantics techniques reduce the number of assumptions that I
make, keeps me aware of assumptions others might make, and helps me
quickly recover andadapt when I make mistakes in the first two
processes :-)

